WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.450
Four customers walk up to the price desk of our post office at the same moment.

2
00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:12.441
Each one needs a lookup in the big price book, and each lookup takes half a second.

3
00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:17.807
So the desk sends all four jobs to the back office, where twenty workers sit idle.

4
00:00:17.807 --> 00:00:20.878
How long until all four customers have an answer?

5
00:00:20.878 --> 00:00:22.884
Make a guess, and hold on to it.

6
00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:30.518
Before we answer, here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:30.518 --> 00:00:34.665
Vert.x runs thousands of connections on a handful of threads.

8
00:00:34.665 --> 00:00:36.704
So who, exactly, runs my code?

9
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:39.105
Start with what we know.

10
00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:41.855
In lesson 3, Netty hired a team of clerks.

11
00:00:41.855 --> 00:00:47.815
Each clerk owns a row of boxes in the wall, for good, and never stops to make a phone call.

12
00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:52.675
Vert.x is a toolkit for network programs that runs on those same clerks.

13
00:00:52.675 --> 00:00:55.705
What it adds is rules about who runs your code.

14
00:00:56.533 --> 00:01:02.160
So picture desks on the counter: a weighing desk, a price desk, a lost-property desk.

15
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.794
Each desk has its own drawers, and a card on top that names its clerk.

16
00:01:06.794 --> 00:01:10.633
The same clerk always serves that desk, one job at a time.

17
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:11.768
Let's map it.

18
00:01:11.768 --> 00:01:17.664
A clerk is an event loop: one thread that serves many connections, one job at a time.

19
00:01:17.664 --> 00:01:21.340
Vert.x starts two per core, so 8 on a 4-core machine.

20
00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:24.201
A desk is a verticle, a class you deploy.

21
00:01:24.201 --> 00:01:29.662
The card is its context: the home of its code, which decides the thread that runs it.

22
00:01:29.662 --> 00:01:32.104
The drawers are the verticle's fields.

23
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:41.172
And the jobs are handlers: your code that Vert.x calls when a request arrives, a timer fires, or a note comes in from another desk.

24
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.916
Now three letters reach the weighing desk at the very same moment.

25
00:01:45.916 --> 00:01:48.228
Does the clerk open all three at once?

26
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:51.101
No.

27
00:01:51.101 --> 00:01:53.380
One, then the next, then the next.

28
00:01:53.380 --> 00:02:00.819
The docs promise it: a handler never runs at the same time as itself, and always on the same event loop thread.

29
00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:05.487
Which makes this the one office where nobody fights over the stapler.

30
00:02:05.487 --> 00:02:12.716
Only one clerk ever opens these drawers, so a field that one context touches needs no lock, and no synchronized.

31
00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:14.445
So far so good.

32
00:02:14.445 --> 00:02:16.966
But look at the price desk's card.

33
00:02:16.966 --> 00:02:17.782
Same clerk.

34
00:02:17.782 --> 00:02:20.080
One clerk serves several desks.

35
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:24.985
Now the price desk does its half second lookup right there, on the clerk's own time.

36
00:02:24.985 --> 00:02:26.436
The clerk is on the phone.

37
00:02:26.436 --> 00:02:30.731
And while he waits, the weighing desk waits too, though it did nothing wrong.

38
00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:39.580
Four customers in a row on one clerk: the lesson's demo took 2,201 milliseconds, all on vert.x-eventloop-thread-0.

39
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.398
Vert.x calls this the golden rule: don't block the event loop.

40
00:02:44.398 --> 00:02:46.203
The docs even give a budget.

41
00:02:46.203 --> 00:02:52.329
One event loop, ten thousand requests a second, and each request gets a tenth of a millisecond.

42
00:02:52.329 --> 00:02:55.488
A half second lookup is five thousand times over.

43
00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:02.598
Vert.x also hires a floor manager, a separate thread that walks past every clerk once a second.

44
00:03:02.598 --> 00:03:06.377
A job that runs over 2 seconds gets a warning in the log.

45
00:03:06.377 --> 00:03:12.277
Over 5 seconds, he adds the stack trace, the list of method calls the thread is stuck in.

46
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.499
In the lesson's demo, a clerk slept for 6 seconds.

47
00:03:16.499 --> 00:03:25.057
The first warning came at 2,773 milliseconds, not 2,000, because the manager only walks past once a second.

48
00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:26.970
And notice what he never does.

49
00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.298
He doesn't tap the clerk on the shoulder.

50
00:03:29.298 --> 00:03:32.988
He writes it down and walks on, and the clerk stays on the phone.

51
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.396
He has a blind spot, too.

52
00:03:35.396 --> 00:03:37.335
He times one job at a time.

53
00:03:37.335 --> 00:03:44.159
Ten calls of 300 milliseconds hold the clerk for 3 seconds, and not one of them gets a warning.

54
00:03:44.967 --> 00:03:50.103
So, the obvious fix: send slow jobs to the back office, with executeBlocking.

55
00:03:50.103 --> 00:03:54.439
Its worker pool has 20 ordinary threads that are allowed to wait.

56
00:03:54.439 --> 00:03:59.643
And the answer comes back to the same desk, so its own clerk writes the reply.

57
00:03:59.867 --> 00:04:03.625
The clerk is free, the warnings stop, and the tests pass.

58
00:04:03.625 --> 00:04:06.987
Back to our four customers and twenty idle workers.

59
00:04:06.987 --> 00:04:07.844
How long now?

60
00:04:10.533 --> 00:04:11.859
2,012 milliseconds.

61
00:04:11.859 --> 00:04:13.604
About the same as before.

62
00:04:13.604 --> 00:04:17.721
And every lookup ran on one thread, vert.x-worker-thread-0.

63
00:04:17.933 --> 00:04:22.287
Twenty workers in the back office, and one of them did all the work.

64
00:04:22.287 --> 00:04:25.809
The other nineteen, I assume, were sorting paper clips.

65
00:04:25.809 --> 00:04:30.227
So the clerk was free, and the customers still waited in single file.

66
00:04:30.227 --> 00:04:30.483
Why?

67
00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.169
One word in the docs.

68
00:04:32.169 --> 00:04:34.827
executeBlocking is ordered by default.

69
00:04:34.827 --> 00:04:38.954
Jobs from the same desk go into one queue, and run in turn.

70
00:04:38.954 --> 00:04:44.760
And every request to one HTTP server, the web server, shares that server's context.

71
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.139
So all four joined the same queue.

72
00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:51.185
Pass false, and the jobs stop waiting for each other.

73
00:04:51.185 --> 00:04:54.932
Four workers pick them up at once: 511 milliseconds.

74
00:04:55.733 --> 00:04:58.035
Here the desk picture breaks a little.

75
00:04:58.035 --> 00:05:02.517
You can also deploy a worker verticle: a desk served from the back office.

76
00:05:02.517 --> 00:05:06.514
It may get a different worker for each job, but never two at once.

77
00:05:06.514 --> 00:05:09.179
One job at a time, without one fixed thread.

78
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.035
Here is the fix in code, in Java and in Kotlin.

79
00:05:13.035 --> 00:05:21.043
The slow lookup goes to executeBlocking, and the second argument, false, says these calls don't need to wait for each other.

80
00:05:21.867 --> 00:05:23.242
So, who runs my code?

81
00:05:23.242 --> 00:05:29.335
The clerk named on its desk's card, one job at a time, which is why the drawers need no lock.

82
00:05:29.335 --> 00:05:31.104
Don't make that clerk wait.

83
00:05:31.104 --> 00:05:36.869
And when you send work to the back office, pass false, or it still waits in single file.

84
00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:37.878
Quick check.

85
00:05:37.878 --> 00:05:43.779
A desk sends mark weighed, then mark shipped, for the same parcel, through executeBlocking.

86
00:05:43.779 --> 00:05:45.205
Should you pass false?

87
00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:48.087
No.

88
00:05:48.087 --> 00:05:51.709
Run them at once, and shipped could finish before weighed.

89
00:05:51.709 --> 00:05:54.144
That's why ordered is the safe default.

90
00:05:54.144 --> 00:05:59.639
Pass false only for jobs that don't depend on each other, like the price desk's lookups.
